<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-minimal-right-ideals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Minimal right ideals</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            We gather some results on minimal right ideals in algebras that will be used in the proof of Artin-Wedderburn's theorem. We will follow <xref ref="bib-Pierce"/>.
        </p>
        <p>
            We assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity, and <m>A</m> is an <m>R</m>-algebra.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-right-ideals-in-product-algebras">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A=A_1\times A_2</m> be a product of <m>R</m>-algebras, and <m>M</m> be a right ideal of <m>A_1</m>. Then we have the following:
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>M</m> is a right ideal of <m>A</m>;
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\End{A}{M}=\End{A_1}{M}</m>;
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\Hom{A}{M}{A}=\Hom{A}{M}{A_1}=\Hom{A_1}{M}{A_1}</m>;
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>S\left(M_{A_1}\right)=S\left(M_A\right)</m>;
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>M</m> is a minimal right ideal of <m>A_1</m> if and only if it is a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m>;
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Every minimal right ideal of <m>A</m> is a minimal right ideal of either <m>A_1</m> or <m>A_2</m>.  
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <corollary xml:id="cor-finite-products-of-semisimple-algebras-is-semisimple">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>A_i</m> for <m>i\in\{1,2,\ldots,n\}</m> are semisimple algebras then, <m>\prod_{i=1}^nA_i</m> is a semisimple algebra.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-technical-lemma">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>N</m> is a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m>, and <m>a\in A</m> then, either <m>aN=0</m> or <m>aN</m> is a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m> such that <m>aN\simeq N</m> as <m>A</m>-modules.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Let <m>N</m> be a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m> and <m>a\in A</m>. If <m>aN=0</m> we are done. Otherwise, let us show that <m>aN</m> is a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m> isomorphic to <m>N</m>. Consider the surjective morphism of right <m>A</m>-modules given by left multiplication by <m>a</m>:
            <me>
                f_a:N\to aN,\quad x\mapsto ax.
            </me>
            Since <m>N</m> is minimal, the kernel of <m>f_a</m> is either <m>0</m> or <m>N</m>. The latter cannot happen since we are assuming that <m>aN\neq 0</m>. Thus, <m>f_a</m> is an isomorphism of right <m>A</m>-modules and so, <m>aN\simeq N</m>. In particular, since <m>N</m> is simple, so is <m>aN</m>, and hence it is a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m>.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-minimal-nilpotent-ideal-implies-in-the-radical">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>N</m> is a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m> such that <m>N^k=0</m> for some <m>k\in\mathbb{N}</m>. Then, for any simple <m>A</m>-module <m>P</m> we have <m>PN=0</m> and furthermore, <m>N\subseteq\rad{A}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <proof>
        <p>
           The module <m>PN</m> is a submodule of the simple (nonzero) module <m>P</m>, so it is either <m>0</m> or <m>P</m>. If <m>PN=P</m>, then <m>0=PN^k=P</m>, contradiction. Thus, <m>PN=0</m>. Since this holds for any simple <m>A</m>-module <m>P</m>, we have <m>N\subseteq\rad{A}</m>, as <m>P\simeq A_A/\mathfrak{m}</m> for some maximal right ideal.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-equivalent-conditions-on-minimal-ideals-of-semisimple-algebras">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A</m> be a semisimple algbera and <m>M,N</m> be minimal right ideals of <m>A</m>. The following are equivalent:
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            As <m>A</m>-modules, <m>M\simeq N</m>;
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>MN\neq 0</m>;
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            there is an element <m>a\in A</m> such that <m>aN=M</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </proposition>
    <proof>
        <p>
            Let <m>M N\neq 0</m>. Suppose that <m>m\in M</m> is such that <m>mN\neq 0</m>. By <xref ref="lem-technical-lemma"/>, we have that <m>mN\simeq N</m>. Since <m>mN\subseteq mA\subseteq M</m> and <m>M</m> is simple, we have <m>mN=M</m>. Thus, (2) implies (3).
        </p>
        <p>
            Suppose that there is an element <m>a\in A</m> such that <m>aN=M</m>. By <xref ref="lem-technical-lemma"/>, we have <m>aN\simeq N</m>, so <m>M\simeq N</m>. Thus, (3) implies (1).
        </p>
    </proof>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-another-technical-lemma">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A</m> be a semisimple algebra, and <m>A_A=N_1\oplus N_2\oplus\cdots\oplus N_m</m> with each <m>N_i</m> a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m>. If <m>M</m> is a minimal right ideal of <m>A</m>, then there exists an index <m>i</m> such that <m>M\simeq N_i</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <proof>
        <p>
            By <xref ref="cor-semisimple-implies-existence-of-complement"/>, there exists a right ideal <m>Q</m> of <m>A</m> such that <m>A_A=M\oplus Q</m> because <m>A_A</m> is semisimple. The module <m>Q</m> is semisimple <m>A</m>-module (see <xref ref="cor-quotient-and-submodule-of-semisimple-module-is-semisimple"/>). Result now follows from <xref ref="thm-Jordan-Holder-for-modules">Jordan-Hölder theorem</xref>.
        </p>
    </proof>
    <p>
        The following corollary follows from <xref ref="prop-modules-over-semisimple-algebra"/> and <xref ref="cor-number-of-isomorphism-classes-of-simple-A-modules"/>.
    </p>
    <corollary xml:id="cor-number-of-isomorphism-classes-of-simple-A-modules">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>A</m> is a semisimple algebra then, the number of isomorphism classes of simple <m>A</m>-modules is finite.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </corollary>
</section>